course: dictionary
lesson: 2300
title: Old-fashioned exclamations
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words are called interjections. They are short words that show a sudden feeling, like surprise or alarm, and they often stand alone. «Cripes» is one of them: you say it when something makes you jump.
explain: Read this sentence: «"Cripes" is an old-fashioned mild word used instead of "Christ".» A mild word is a gentle one. Writers use «cripes» when they want a shout of surprise without a stronger word. A softer word used in place of a stronger one is called a euphemism.
explain: You will find «cripes» in old comics, school stories and in what grandparents say. Context helps you here: if a character shouts it after something unexpected, the feeling is surprise, alarm or annoyance. Check by asking what the character is feeling.
fact: An interjection is a short word that shows a sudden feeling.
fact: «Cripes» is a cry of surprise, alarm or annoyance.
fact: A euphemism is a mild word used in place of a stronger or rude one.
fact: «Cripes» is old-fashioned, so you will mostly meet it in old books, comics and from grandparents.
fact: «Cripes» is a safe, mild word, and it is not tied to one region of the English-speaking world.
exercise: Ruby sees her dropped vase in pieces and shouts, «Cripes!» What is she feeling? | *Surprised and alarmed | Hungry | Sleepy | Bored
exercise: «Cripes» is a soft stand-in for which stronger word? | Crispy | *Christ | Crumbs | Cricket
exercise: Which best describes «cripes»? | *An old-fashioned, mild exclamation | A kind of biscuit | A rude word to avoid | A modern text message
test: Grandad sees you have grown a foot and cries, «Cripes, you've shot up!» What does cripes show? | Boredom | Tiredness | *Surprise | Anger
test: What is a euphemism? | A very long word | A word that means very old | *A softer word used instead of a stronger one | A question word
exam: The word «cripes» is an interjection. What does that mean? | A word that means very old | *A short word that shows a sudden feeling | A word that names a place | A word that joins two sentences together
